# `.env` alone is an EXACT name match. It does not cover `.env.production`,
# `.env.development` or `.env.test` — all of which Vite loads by convention and
# python-dotenv is routinely pointed at. Measured 2026-09-06: `git check-ignore`
# reported backend/.env.production and frontend/.env.production NOT IGNORED, i.e.
# one `git add -A` away from a real key in a public repo (S1, S9).
.env
.env.*
# The template is the one env file that belongs in the repo. It carries the KEY
# NAMES and no values; keep it tracked, and keep the placeholder a placeholder.
!.env.example
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
*.egg
.venv/
venv/
env/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
*.cover
data/qdrant/
output/
*.log

# Uploaded course material — NEVER commit (S7).
# `data/qdrant/` alone was not enough: a student's or professor's source decks land
# in data/<course name>/ and were fully stageable by `git add -A`. This repository is
# public; a professor's unpublished slides are not ours to publish. Caught 2026-08-30
# when 14 real lecture PDFs (30 MB) showed as untracked rather than ignored.
# If the §14.6 pre-baked demo collection is ever committed, do it deliberately with
# `git add -f` after the S9 secret scan — never by relaxing this rule.
data/
